    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Births New Adventure Scooter Family, Three RW Models That Look More Like Motorcycles\nThere Is One Chinese Automaker That Is Inching Closer to the U.S. Market\n11 photos\nPhoto: Jaecoo\nChinese automakers are knocking at the doors of the American car market one way or another, and Canada seems to be the password to entry. Chery Automobile Co. is exploring local manufacturing options in Canada as it prepares to launch vehicle sales there. Direct access to the U.S. market remains uncertain due to strict tariffs and software restrictions.\nChery is inching closer to its debut on the Canadian market, scheduled to take place in early 2027. In the meantime, it is struggling to find a shortcut into the United States. Executive Vice President Guibing Zhang explained that Chery is even considering setting up a local plant or taking over idle plant capacity and working with partners to achieve this target.\nWhile a presence in Canada raises massive concerns about the already existing tensions with Washington, which imposes a prohibitive 100% tariff on Chinese EVs, Zhang noted that Canada is valuable enough for the automaker to justify local manufacturing, even if access to the U.S. market is never possible.\nHowever, Canadian manufacturing would require meeting local labor standards, building domestic supply chains and offering secure software systems. Meanwhile, Canada shifted its trade policy by canceling its 100% tax on Chinese vehicles, which opens the door for high-tech cars at affordable prices.\nUnder a trade agreement brokered by Prime Minister Mark Carney, Canada replaced the tariff with a targeted quota system, which allows up to 49,000 EVs or hybrids built in China into Canada annually.\nPhoto: Jaecoo For these vehicles, Chinese automakers would pay the baseline tariff rate of only 6.1% instead of the huge 100% or over. The number limits of vehicles will step up progressively to roughly 63,000 units per year by 2030.\nThis move marks a strict policy departure from the United States. In the meantime, American-built vehicles entering the Canadian market will continue to face a 25% tariff, prompting automakers like Tesla to supply Canada from Shanghai and Germany instead of its U.S. plants.\nThis is the context in which Chery is launching its export-focused sub-brands, Omoda and Jaecoo, in Canada through its newly registered entity, Omoda & Jaecoo Canada Inc. The launch was originally scheduled for late 2026. Now, Chery Vice President Charlie Zhang has just confirmed that the brand launch is scheduled for early 2027, as reported by Bloomberg.\nIn the meantime, the company is planning to open roughly a dozen dealerships across Quebec, Ontario, and British Columbia, aiming for 6,000 unit sales in its first full year on the Canadian market.\nPhoto: Omoda Test vehicles operating with Ontario manufacturer plates have already been spotted around the Greater Toronto Area. The Omoda 9 , which is a plug-in hybrid with 450 horsepower, and the Jaecoo J5 electric SUV will be two key players on the Canadian market.\nPricing has yet to be disclosed. However, the Jaecoo J5 is expected to start at around 36,000 Canadian dollars, which translates to roughly US$25,500. In the meantime, the Omoda 9 should kick off at approximately 48,000 Canadian dollars ($34,000). The two models are already on the market in Europe and Australia .\nChinese-manufactured vehicles do not qualify for Canada’s federal Electric Vehicle Affordability Program (EVAP), which brings incentives of up to 5,000 Canadian dollars to electric vehicles.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChery Chery Omoda omoda Jaecoo J5 jaecoo\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT November Mini GT Preorder Reveals Six Fast Cars: One of Them Is a New Casting!",
